      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224893",
      ":question/problem": "Prove the following theorem:\n\n> Let $a$ and $b$ be integers and $n$ be a positive integer. If $gcd(a, n) = k > 1$ with $k\u2223b$, then the congruence $ax\u2261b(modn)$ has exactly $k$ distinct solutions modulo $n$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: We are given that $gcd(a, n) = k > 1$ and $k\u2223b$.\n> $L2$: Then, $\u2203a_{1}, n_{1} \u2208 Z$, such that $a = a_{1}k, n = n_{1}k$.\n> $L3$: And, in turn, $\u2203b_{1} \u2208 Z, b = b_{1}k$.\n> $L4$: Since $k = gcd(a, n)$, we have that $gcd(a_{1}, n_{1}) = 1$.\n> $L5$: Therefore, $\u2203u, v \u2208 Z$ such that $a_{1}u + n_{1}v = 1$.\n> $L6$: We can rewrite this equality as $a_{1}(b_{1}u) + n_{1}(b_{1}v) = b_{1}$.\n> $L7$: So, we have that $a_{1}(b_{1}u) + n_{1}(b_{1}v)\u2261b_{1}(modn_{1})$.\n> $L8$: This implies that $a_{1}(b_{1}u)\u2261b_{1}(modn_{1})$.\n> $L9$: So, $x_{1} = b_{1}u$ is a solution of the congruence $a_{1}x\u2261b_{1}(modn_{1})$.\n> $L10$: Now, it is true that $a_{1}x_{i}\u2261b_{1}(modn_{1})$, for $x_{i} = x_{1} + (i - 1)n_{1}, 1 \\le i \\le k$.\n> $L11$: Therefore, $(ka_{1})x_{i}\u2261(kb_{1})(modkn_{1})$.\n> $L12$: Thus, $ax_{i}\u2261b(modn)$.\n> $L13$: This means that $x_{i}$ is solution for original congruence $ax\u2261b(modn)$ for each $1 \\le i \\le k$.\n> $L14$: Now, suppose that $i, j \u2208 {1, \u2026, k - 1}, i \\ne j$ and $x_{i}\u2261x_{j}(modn)$.\n> $L15$: Then, $x_{1} + (i - 1)n_{1}\u2261x_{1} + (j - 1)n_{1}(modn)$.\n> $L16$: So, $(i - 1)n_{1}\u2261(j - 1)n_{1}(modn_{1}k)$.\n> $L17$: And we get that $i\u2261j(modk)$.\n> $L18$: Therefore, $i = j$, which is a contradiction.\n> $L19$: This means that the solutions $x_{i}$ are distinct and mutually non-congruent modulo $n$.\n> $L20$: Finally, we conclude that the congruence has exactly $k$ distinct solutions modulo $n$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from {{field-1}} in line $L1$ by {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L8$ follows from line {{field-3}} by the fact that {{field-4}}.\n\nLine $L17$ follows from line $L16$ by the {{field-5}}."
